Root
  Tool: Commercial, open-source, built-in, or publicly available software that could be used by a defender, pen tester, red teamer, or an adversary. This category includes both software that generally is not found on an enterprise system as well as software generally available as part of an operating system that is already present in an environment. Examples include PsExec, Metasploit, Mimikatz, as well as Windows utilities such as Net, netstat, Tasklist, etc.
    Built-in System Utilities - Native operating system tools and commands
      Net Commands (net.exe, net1.exe) - Network and user account management
        Use "net user" to enumerate local and domain user accounts, identify administrators, and gather user information
        Use "net group" to enumerate domain groups, particularly privileged groups like "Domain Admins"
        Use "net localgroup" to view and modify local group memberships for privilege escalation
        Use "net accounts" to query password policies and account lockout thresholds
        Use "net share" to enumerate network shares and identify potential data sources
        Use "net use" to map network drives and establish network connections
        Use "net view" to discover network resources and enumerate domain computers
        Use "net start/stop" to manipulate services for persistence or defense evasion
        Use "net session" to identify active sessions and connected users
        Use "net time" to query domain time servers and system time synchronization
        Use "net config" to gather system configuration details and server roles
        Use "net statistics" to gather system uptime and service statistics
        Use "net computer" to add/remove computers from domain for persistence
        Use "net trust" to enumerate domain trust relationships
        Use "net file" to identify and manipulate opened files on shares
        Use "net pause" to temporarily suspend services without stopping them
        Use "net continue" to resume previously paused services
        Use "net user /domain" specifically for domain user account creation and manipulation
        Use "net group /domain" for domain-level group membership modifications
        Use "net localgroup administrators" to add backdoor admin accounts
        Use "net config workstation" to gather information about workstation configuration and domain membership
        Use "net print" to enumerate network printers and print servers
        Use "net name" to display computer names and aliases
        Use "net password" to change or reset user passwords remotely
        Use "net send" for network messaging (on legacy systems)
        Use "net logon" to verify domain controller accessibility and authentication
        Use "net help" to explore available commands while avoiding detection
        Use "net1 command /?" for detailed help without spawning net.exe
        Use "net config server" to identify server roles and configurations
        Use "net user guest" to manipulate built-in guest account settings
