      Hydra - Network login cracker and brute force tool
        Web form brute forcing - Configure Hydra for HTTP POST attacks against login forms using custom parameter lists, testing multiple username/password combinations while avoiding detection
        SSH credential attacks - Launch parallel SSH brute force attempts with customized wordlists, timing controls, and port specifications
        FTP service exploitation - Execute dictionary attacks against FTP servers using both anonymous and known username lists with common password patterns
        RDP authentication attacks - Perform distributed brute force attacks against Windows Remote Desktop services with specific username lists and password complexity rules
        Database login attacks - Target MySQL, PostgreSQL, and Oracle databases with credential stuffing attacks using known default credentials and common combinations
        SMTP authentication cracking - Test mail server security by attempting authentication with common username patterns and password lists
        SMB share access attempts - Launch brute force attacks against Windows file shares and domain controllers using gathered username lists
        VNC password recovery - Execute password guessing attacks against VNC servers using both dictionary and incremental attack methods
        Telnet service exploitation - Perform credential attacks against legacy telnet services with custom timing and retry parameters
        LDAP directory attacks - Conduct brute force attempts against LDAP authentication services using gathered organizational information
        Cisco device authentication attacks - Target network infrastructure using specific Cisco protocol modules with customized timing to avoid lockouts
        IPsec/IKE gateway attacks - Perform brute force attacks against VPN concentrators and IPsec gateways using pre-shared keys
        Network proxy authentication bypass - Attack proxy servers using gathered credentials and custom rules for authentication mechanisms
        POP3/IMAP mail server attacks - Target email services with combination of username enumeration and password attacks
        Custom module development - Create specialized modules for proprietary protocols or applications using Hydra's module framework
        Distributed attack coordination - Configure multiple Hydra instances across different IP addresses to avoid detection and blocking
        RADIUS server authentication attacks - Target network access control systems using gathered user information
        WebDAV service exploitation - Attack web-based file sharing and collaboration systems using credential lists
        IRC server authentication attacks - Target chat and communication servers using nickname and operator credential lists
        API endpoint authentication attacks - Configure custom HTTP attacks against REST APIs with specific header and parameter requirements
        Password list optimization - Process and customize wordlists using statistical analysis and target organization information
        Two-factor authentication bypass attempts - Configure specialized attacks against 2FA implementations that have fallback authentication
        Cloud service authentication attacks - Target cloud platform login endpoints with specific rulesets for cloud service providers
        Load balancer authentication bypass - Coordinate attacks to handle load-balanced services using session persistence techniques
        Kerberos authentication attacks - Configure specialized modules for attacking Kerberos-based authentication systems
        SNMP community string attacks - Perform brute force attacks against network management interfaces using common string lists
        Automation framework integration - Create scripts to integrate Hydra with other penetration testing tools for automated workflows
        Rate limiting evasion - Implement advanced timing controls and proxy rotation to bypass defensive rate limiting
        Service fingerprinting combination - Integrate service detection with targeted protocol-specific attacks
        Session persistence attacks - Maintain and reuse valid sessions while testing different credential combinations
        Enterprise SSO attacks - Configure attacks against Single Sign-On systems with custom form parameters and federation protocols
        Credential output processing - Implement automated parsing and validation of successful credentials for immediate use in lateral movement
        Mainframe access attacks - Target mainframe terminal emulation services using specialized username formats and password rules
        Password policy analysis - Use successful credentials to derive password patterns and generate targeted wordlists
        Multi-service credential verification - Test discovered credentials across different services to identify password reuse
        Nested authentication attacks - Configure sequential attacks for systems requiring multiple layers of authentication
        Backup system authentication - Target backup service interfaces and management consoles using common vendor credentials
        Credential harvesting integration - Combine Hydra attacks with previously harvested credentials from other sources
        Jump box targeting - Configure specialized attacks against intermediate access systems and bastion hosts
        Logging evasion techniques - Implement methods to avoid generating detection patterns in target system logs
