        POP3 authentication testing: Use "-M pop3" module to test email retrieval credentials
        IMAP mailbox authentication: Deploy "-M imap" module for testing email account access
        CVS repository authentication: Utilize "-M cvs" module for testing version control system access
        REXEC service testing: Execute "-M rexec" module for remote execution service authentication
        RLOGIN credential testing: Use "-M rlogin" module for remote login authentication attacks
        VMAUTHD service testing: Apply "-M vmauthd" module for virtual machine authentication testing
        Parallel host scanning: Configure multiple host testing with "-H" flag and hosts.txt file
        Custom username/password combination testing: Use "-C" flag with combo file for specific credential pairs
        Rate-limited authentication attempts: Implement "-q" flag to control request timing and avoid detection
        Proxy-enabled testing: Configure "-x" flag to route authentication attempts through proxy servers
        Resume interrupted sessions: Use "-Z" flag to continue from last attempt in case of connection drops
        Custom port testing: Implement "-n" flag to specify non-standard ports for service testing
        Debug mode execution: Deploy "-d" flag for verbose output and troubleshooting authentication attempts
        Custom retry attempts: Configure "-r" and "-R" flags to control retry count and timing for failed attempts
        Output formatting: Use "-O" flag to specify custom output file format for result analysis
        Password list generation: Create dynamic password lists using built-in password mangling rules
        Custom authentication timeout: Set "-t" flag to adjust connection timeouts for slow responding services
        Failed attempt logging: Configure "-F" flag to log failed attempts separately for analysis
        Host-based authentication limits: Use "-L" flag to restrict concurrent tests per target
        SSL/TLS encryption testing: Enable "-S" flag for testing SSL/TLS encrypted service authentication
      DNSRecon - DNS enumeration script
        Perform standard DNS enumeration using -d option to discover A, AAAA, MX, NS, SOA, and other common record types: dnsrecon -d target.com
        Execute zone transfer attempts against target DNS servers: dnsrecon -d target.com -t axfr
        Conduct reverse lookup on IP range to identify additional hosts: dnsrecon -r 192.168.1.0/24 -n 8.8.8.8
        Brute force subdomains using built-in or custom wordlists: dnsrecon -d target.com -D /path/to/wordlist.txt -t brt
        Enumerate SRV records for common services: dnsrecon -d target.com -t srv
        Cache snooping against target DNS servers: dnsrecon -t snoop -n <DNS_Server> -D domains.txt
        Google enumeration to discover additional subdomains: dnsrecon -d target.com -t goo
        Use WHOIS queries to gather domain registration info: dnsrecon -d target.com -w
        Perform zone walking on NSEC records for DNSSEC enabled domains: dnsrecon -d target.com -t zonewalk
        Generate detailed reports in XML or JSON format: dnsrecon -d target.com --xml dnsrecon.xml
        Perform DNS enumeration through specific DNS servers: dnsrecon -d target.com -n 8.8.8.8,8.8.4.4
        Use BING search engine for subdomain discovery: dnsrecon -d target.com -t bing
        Conduct IPv6 reverse lookups: dnsrecon -r 2001:db8::/32 -n 2001:db8::1
        Enumerate wildcard DNS configurations: dnsrecon -d target.com -t std --wildcard
        Perform threaded brute force with custom timeout: dnsrecon -d target.com -t brt -T 20 --lifetime 10
        Generate CSV output for integration with other tools: dnsrecon -d target.com --csv output.csv
        Use custom record types for enumeration: dnsrecon -d target.com -t std --type TXT,SPF
        Perform recursive subdomain brute forcing: dnsrecon -d target.com -t brt -D wordlist.txt --recursive
        Query specific name servers for zone information: dnsrecon -d target.com -t axfr -n ns1.target.com,ns2.target.com
        Utilize TCP instead of UDP for queries: dnsrecon -d target.com --tcp
        Use custom regular expressions for filtering results: dnsrecon -d target.com --regex '^(dev|staging).*'
        Perform geolocation lookups on discovered IPs: dnsrecon -d target.com --geo
        Set specific DNS query timeout values: dnsrecon -d target.com --lifetime 30
        Enumerate with specific DNS record caching: dnsrecon -d target.com --cache
        Use NSEC3 record enumeration for DNSSEC domains: dnsrecon -d target.com -t nsec3
        Perform range scans with custom threads: dnsrecon -r 192.168.0.0/16 -n 8.8.8.8 -T 50
        Filter results based on specific IP ranges: dnsrecon -d target.com --filter-range 192.168.0.0/24
        Use specific DNS protocols (e.g., DoT, DoH): dnsrecon -d target.com --ssl
        Implement custom domain sorting and filtering: dnsrecon -d target.com --sorted
        Save session state for resumed operations: dnsrecon -d target.com --save state.json
