      Hakrawler - Web crawler for gathering URLs
        Basic crawling with default settings: hakrawler -url https://target.com -depth 2
        Extract JavaScript files: hakrawler -url https://target.com -js -depth 3
        Crawl with custom headers to bypass restrictions: hakrawler -h "User-Agent: CustomBot" -url https://target.com
        Use with subfinder for subdomain enumeration: subfinder -d target.com | hakrawler
        Extract URLs from sitemap.xml: hakrawler -url https://target.com/sitemap.xml -sitemap
        Export results to file for analysis: hakrawler -url https://target.com | tee results.txt
        Crawl with authentication: hakrawler -url https://target.com -h "Cookie: session=value"
        Combine with waybackurls: echo "target.com" | waybackurls | hakrawler
        Use custom timeout settings: hakrawler -url https://target.com -timeout 20
        Scope-limited crawling: hakrawler -url https://target.com -scope strict
        Filter unique subdomains: hakrawler -url https://target.com | sort -u | grep sub
        Concurrent crawling with multiple threads: hakrawler -url https://target.com -threads 10
        Extract forms and input fields: hakrawler -url https://target.com -forms
        Chain with nuclei for vulnerability scanning: hakrawler -url https://target.com | nuclei -t templates/
        Use with proxy for traffic inspection: hakrawler -url https://target.com -proxy http://127.0.0.1:8080
        Extract only specific file types: hakrawler -url https://target.com | grep ".pdf"
        Crawl with custom depth and regex patterns: hakrawler -url https://target.com -depth 4 -regex
        Silent mode crawling for stealth: hakrawler -url https://target.com -silent
        Use with rate limiting: hakrawler -url https://target.com -delay 2
        JSON output for automated processing: hakrawler -url https://target.com -json
        Chain with EyeWitness for screenshot capture: hakrawler -url https://target.com | eyewitness --web
        Extract and analyze robots.txt entries: hakrawler -url https://target.com/robots.txt -robots
        Use with custom DNS resolvers: hakrawler -url https://target.com -resolvers custom-resolvers.txt
        Filter out specific file extensions: hakrawler -url https://target.com | grep -v ".png\|.jpg\|.gif"
        Recursive crawling with domain validation: hakrawler -url https://target.com -recursive -domain-validation
        Export results in CSV format: hakrawler -url https://target.com | awk '{print $1,","}' > output.csv
        Use with custom allowed domains list: hakrawler -url https://target.com -allowed-domains domains.txt
        Extract and analyze URL parameters: hakrawler -url https://target.com | grep "?" | cut -d "?" -f2 | sort -u
        Combine with directory bruteforcing: hakrawler -url https://target.com | ffuf -w - -u FUZZ
        Use with custom crawling patterns: hakrawler -url https://target.com -patterns patterns.txt
        Use with fuzzing payloads: hakrawler -url https://target.com | while read url; do ffuf -w payloads.txt -u "$url"
        Extract and validate API endpoints: hakrawler -url https://target.com | grep -i "/api/" | httpx -status-code
        Chain with content discovery tools: hakrawler -url https://target.com | gospider -S - -d 3
        Crawl with specific TLS configuration: hakrawler -url https://target.com -insecure -tls-version 1.2
        Extract and analyze web technologies: hakrawler -url https://target.com | whatweb -a 3
        Use with custom blacklist patterns: hakrawler -url https://target.com -blacklist "admin|login|logout"
        Export findings to Burp Suite: hakrawler -url https://target.com | while read url; do curl "$url" -x 127.0.0.1:8080
        Analyze JavaScript dependencies: hakrawler -url https://target.com -js | grep -i "import\|require"
        Crawl with specific user permissions: hakrawler -url https://target.com -h "Authorization: Bearer token"
        Archive crawled content: hakrawler -url https://target.com | while read url; do wget --mirror "$url"
      Amass-active - Active DNS enumeration mode
        Execute basic active enumeration with default settings:
        amass enum -active -d target.com -o results.txt
        Perform comprehensive DNS brute forcing with custom wordlist:
        amass enum -active -brute -w custom_wordlist.txt -d target.com
        Utilize specific DNS resolvers for enumeration:
        amass enum -active -rf resolvers.txt -d target.com
        Enable ASN tracing and IP collection:
        amass enum -active -asn 12345,67890 -ip -d target.com
        Configure rate limiting for stealth:
        amass enum -active -max-dns-queries 100 -d target.com
        Use alterations for permutation scanning:
        amass enum -active -alts -d target.com
        Enable SSL/TLS certificate grabbing:
        amass enum -active -include-unresolvable -tls -d target.com
        Integrate with external APIs for enhanced enumeration:
        amass enum -active -config config.ini -d target.com
        Target specific network ranges:
        amass enum -active -cidr 192.168.0.0/16 -d target.com
        Generate detailed output with timestamps:
        amass enum -active -json output.json -log amass.log -d target.com
        Perform zone transfer attempts:
        amass enum -active -axfr -d target.com
        Generate visual output for infrastructure mapping:
        amass viz -d3 -d target.com -o visual_map.html
        Execute with specific DNS record types:
        amass enum -active -min-for-recursive 2 -types CNAME,NS,MX,SRV -d target.com
        Integrate with custom scripts for validation:
        amass enum -active -script /path/to/scripts/ -d target.com
        Use domain filtering for specific patterns:
        amass enum -active -exclude-path excludelist.txt -include-path includelist.txt -d target.com
        Enable deep recursive enumeration:
        amass enum -active -max-depth 3 -d target.com
        Perform active enumeration with proxy support:
        amass enum -active -proxy "socks5://127.0.0.1:9050" -d target.com
        Execute with specific timeout settings:
        amass enum -active -timeout 10 -retry 3 -d target.com
        Use DNS wildcard detection and filtering:
        amass enum -active -check-wildcard -d target.com
        Enable active port scanning for discovered hosts:
        amass enum -active -ports 80,443,8080 -d target.com
        Execute with DNS amplification protection:
        amass enum -active -max-dns-name-len 25 -d target.com
        Perform organization-specific enumeration:
        amass enum -active -org "Target Organization" -d target.com
        Use multiple concurrent enumeration:
        amass enum -active -concurrent 2 -d domain1.com,domain2.com
        Generate graphing database output:
        amass enum -active -gremlin output.grem -d target.com
        Implement custom DNS transport:
        amass enum -active -dl domain-list.txt -transport-dial "custom" -d target.com
        Execute with specific source addresses:
        amass enum -active -source-ip 192.168.1.2 -d target.com
        Use DNS modification tracking:
        amass track -config config.ini -d target.com -last 2
        Perform reverse DNS sweeping:
        amass enum -active -reverse -ip -cidr 192.168.0.0/24
        Execute with local database caching:
        amass enum -active -dir ./amass_cache -d target.com
        Generate machine-readable output for automation:
        amass enum -active -json-line -df domains.txt > results.jsonl
      DNSMap - Subdomain brute-forcing tool
        Basic subdomain enumeration using default wordlist: dnsmap target.com
        Custom wordlist scanning: dnsmap target.com -w /path/to/wordlist.txt
        Save results to file: dnsmap target.com -r /path/to/results.txt
        Delay between requests to avoid detection: dnsmap target.com -d 1000
        Filter false positives using built-in verification: dnsmap target.com -i
