      Tcpdump - Command-line packet analyzer for network traffic capture
        Basic network sniffing: Capture all packets on interface eth0 with command 'tcpdump -i eth0 -w capture.pcap' to collect unfiltered traffic for later analysis
        Target specific hosts: Use 'tcpdump host [target_ip] -w target.pcap' to monitor traffic to/from specific systems of interest
        Protocol-specific monitoring: Execute 'tcpdump port 53' or 'tcpdump port 80' to capture DNS or HTTP traffic for credential harvesting
        Advanced filtering: Implement 'tcpdump tcp[tcpflags] & tcp-syn != 0' to detect network scanning activities
        Credential capture: Filter for authentication traffic with 'tcpdump port 445 or port 389' to capture NTLM/Kerberos authentication attempts
        Real-time packet inspection: Use 'tcpdump -A' to display packet contents in ASCII for immediate analysis of clear-text protocols
        Write to multiple files: Execute 'tcpdump -w file_%H_%M' to create rotating capture files for long-term monitoring
        Stealth capture: Run 'tcpdump -n -i any -s0 -w /tmp/hidden.pcap' with minimal output to avoid detection
        Extract sensitive data: Filter with 'tcpdump port 25 or port 110' to capture email traffic and credentials
        Monitor internal routing: Use 'tcpdump ip proto \\icmp' to capture routing and network topology information
        Capture specific packet sizes: Use 'tcpdump len > 1000' to identify potential file transfers or data exfiltration
        Filter by TCP flags: Execute 'tcpdump "tcp[tcpflags] == tcp-fin"' to detect connection terminations and network mapping
        VLAN monitoring: Implement 'tcpdump -i eth0 vlan' to capture traffic across virtual networks
        Payload pattern matching: Use 'tcpdump -X' combined with grep to search for specific strings in packet payloads
        Time-based capture: Execute 'tcpdump -G 3600 -w hourly_%Y%m%d%H.pcap' for scheduled monitoring periods
        Buffer size manipulation: Run 'tcpdump -B 524288' to handle high-speed captures without packet loss
        MAC address filtering: Use 'tcpdump ether host [MAC]' to track specific devices regardless of IP changes
        Decrypt SSL/TLS: Combine with SSLKEYLOGFILE environment variable to capture decrypted HTTPS traffic
        Advanced protocol analysis: Use 'tcpdump "tcp[((tcp[12:1] & 0xf0) >> 2):4]"' to extract specific protocol fields
        Custom BPF filtering: Create complex Berkeley Packet Filter expressions for targeted capture scenarios
        Count-based captures: Use 'tcpdump -c 1000' to capture specific number of packets for targeted analysis
        Fragment analysis: Execute 'tcpdump "ip[6] & 0x20 != 0"' to identify and monitor fragmented packets
        Bandwidth analysis: Implement 'tcpdump -w - | tee capture.pcap | tcpdump -r - -n -v' for real-time bandwidth monitoring
        Interface bonding capture: Use 'tcpdump -i bond0' to monitor aggregated network interfaces
        Multicast traffic analysis: Run 'tcpdump ip multicast' to capture group communication patterns
        Time-window analysis: Execute 'tcpdump -G 60 -w "%Y-%m-%d_%H:%M:%S.pcap"' for precise time-window captures
        ARP monitoring: Use 'tcpdump arp' to track network mapping and potential ARP spoofing
        Packet truncation: Implement 'tcpdump -s 96' to capture only headers for covert monitoring
        Ring buffer implementation: Use 'tcpdump -C 100 -W 5' for rotating buffer of capture files
        Raw packet capture: Execute 'tcpdump -w raw.pcap -s 0 "ip proto \\tcp"' for complete packet contents
        Remote capture: Use 'ssh user@host tcpdump -w - | tcpdump -r -' for stealthy remote monitoring
        Promiscuous mode toggle: Execute 'tcpdump -e promisc' to capture all visible traffic regardless of destination
        Custom output format: Implement 'tcpdump -l | awk '{print $2,$3}'' for specific field extraction
        Geo-location tracking: Combine with 'tcpdump and geoiplookup' for tracking traffic sources
        ICMP type filtering: Use 'tcpdump "icmp[icmptype] == 8"' to monitor specific ICMP message types
        IPv6 traffic isolation: Execute 'tcpdump ip6' for monitoring IPv6-specific communications
        Time offset analysis: Run 'tcpdump -tt' for timestamp analysis of packet sequences
        Packet direction filtering: Use 'tcpdump -Q in' or 'tcpdump -Q out' for directional analysis
        Service discovery: Implement 'tcpdump port not 22' to identify non-SSH services
        Compressed capture: Execute 'tcpdump -w - | gzip > capture.pcap.gz' for storage-efficient capture
        Kernel buffer size modification: Use 'tcpdump -B 4096000' for high-speed network capture without drops
        Parallel capture sessions: Execute multiple tcpdump instances with different filters for distributed monitoring
        Port range monitoring: Use 'tcpdump portrange 100-200' to capture specific service ranges
        Layer 2 protocol analysis: Implement 'tcpdump ether proto 0x88cc' for LLDP monitoring
        Statistical sampling: Run 'tcpdump -p sample 1/1000' for traffic sampling in high-volume networks
        DNS query extraction: Use 'tcpdump -vvv -s 0 port 53' for detailed DNS analysis
        Interface failover capture: Configure capture across redundant interfaces with scripted failover
        Time correlation: Execute 'tcpdump with ntpd logs' for temporal analysis of network events
        Binary protocol analysis: Use 'tcpdump -X -s0' for hex+ASCII output of binary protocols
        Load balancer monitoring: Implement capture before and after load balancer for traffic distribution analysis
