      TCPDump - Command-line packet analyzer
        Capture all network traffic on specific interface with command "tcpdump -i <interface> -w output.pcap", store in PCAP format for later analysis
        Monitor specific host traffic using filters like "tcpdump host <target_IP> -n" to track communications to/from target
        Capture sensitive protocol traffic with "tcpdump port <port_number>" focusing on common ports (80,443,21,22,3389)
        Extract credentials and authentication data using "tcpdump -A -s0 port http or port ftp or port smtp or port imap or port pop3"
        Monitor internal network segment using "tcpdump net <network_address>/<subnet_mask>" to identify lateral movement
        Capture DNS queries with "tcpdump -i any port 53" to track domain resolution and potential C2 traffic
        Use advanced filtering with Berkeley Packet Filter syntax to target specific protocols and packet contents
        Implement stealth capture by disabling DNS resolution (-n), suppressing timestamp (-t), and minimizing output (-q)
        Rotate capture files with -W and -C options to manage large-scale collection without filling disk space
        Export captured traffic through encrypted channels or split into smaller chunks for discrete exfiltration
        Capture specific application traffic using "tcpdump port <app_port> and tcp[tcpflags] & tcp-push != 0" to focus on data-carrying packets
        Monitor ICMP traffic with "tcpdump icmp" to detect ping sweeps and network mapping attempts
        Implement bandwidth-based triggers using "-B buffer_size" to capture burst traffic and avoid dropped packets
