      Memory DD - Command line memory acquisition tool
        Acquire full physical memory dump using "memdump -p output.dump" command for complete system memory capture
        Extract specific process memory using "memdump -p [PID] output.dump" to target individual processes
        Use built-in compression with "memdump -z output.dump.gz" to reduce dump file size during acquisition
        Implement remote memory acquisition by specifying network share path "memdump -p \\server\share\output.dump"
        Perform selective memory region dumping using address range parameters "memdump -p -r 0x00000000-0xFFFFFFFF output.dump"
        Execute memory acquisition with minimum system impact using low priority mode "memdump -p -l output.dump"
        Capture kernel memory specifically with "memdump -k kernel.dump" for kernel-level analysis
        Use raw output format with "memdump -p -r output.raw" for compatibility with other analysis tools
        Implement automated acquisition through scheduled tasks or scripts for periodic memory dumps
        Configure crash dump mode using "memdump -c crash.dump" to capture system state during crashes
        Use memory dump verification with "memdump -p -v output.dump" to ensure integrity of captured data
        Implement batch processing with multiple PIDs using "memdump -p pid1,pid2,pid3 output.dump"
        Export memory dumps in specific formats (DD, Raw, DMP) using "memdump -p -f [format] output.dump"
        Configure custom buffer sizes with "memdump -p -b [size] output.dump" for optimized performance
        Generate acquisition logs with "memdump -p -l log.txt output.dump" for forensic documentation
        Execute memory dumps with specific user privileges using "memdump -p -u [username] output.dump"
        Implement memory filtering using "memdump -p -x [exclude] output.dump" to skip specific regions
        Create split dumps using "memdump -p -s [size] output.dump" for handling large memory captures
        Use quiet mode with "memdump -p -q output.dump" for stealth operation
        Configure timeout parameters using "memdump -p -t [seconds] output.dump" for controlled acquisition
        Implement memory acquisition with hardware-based write blocking using "memdump -p -hw output.dump"
        Configure memory dump encryption with "memdump -p -e [algorithm] output.dump" for secure storage
        Use pattern-based memory scanning with "memdump -p -m [pattern] output.dump" for targeted acquisition
        Execute parallel dumps across multiple targets using "memdump -p -parallel [targets] output.dump"
        Implement resume capability with "memdump -p -r output.dump" for interrupted acquisitions
        Configure custom metadata inclusion using "memdump -p -meta [fields] output.dump"
        Use memory range exclusion lists with "memdump -p -exclude-file list.txt output.dump"
        Implement pre-acquisition memory space analysis using "memdump -p -analyze output.dump"
        Configure bandwidth throttling with "memdump -p -throttle [rate] output.dump"
        Use signature-based memory region identification with "memdump -p -sig [signatures] output.dump"
        Configure live memory differential dumping using "memdump -p -diff previous.dump output.dump"
        Implement memory dump chain of custody with "memdump -p -coc [details] output.dump"
        Use emergency rapid acquisition mode with "memdump -p -rapid output.dump" for time-critical scenarios
