      Crlfuzz - CRLF injection scanner
        Basic CRLF scan using default wordlist: Install Crlfuzz via Go, set target URL, execute 'crlfuzz -u "http://example.com"' to scan single URL for CRLF vulnerabilities
        Mass URL scanning: Import list of target URLs from file, use 'crlfuzz -l urls.txt' to perform bulk CRLF injection testing across multiple endpoints
        Custom payload testing: Create and use custom CRLF injection payloads file, execute with '-p payloads.txt' to test specific injection patterns
        Concurrent scanning: Utilize threading options (-c flag) to increase scan speed, adjust workers count based on target infrastructure
        Output filtering: Use '-s' flag to only display successful CRLF injection points, export results to file for further analysis
        Silent mode operation: Execute tool with '-silent' flag for stealth scanning, reduce network footprint and avoid detection
        Proxy chain deployment: Configure tool with proxy settings to obscure source IP, route scans through multiple proxies
        Custom header injection: Test specific HTTP headers for CRLF vulnerability using '-H' flag with custom header definitions
        Rate limiting bypass: Implement delayed requests using '-d' flag to avoid rate limiting and WAF detection
        Response validation: Use '-mc' flag to filter responses by status code, focus on identifying exploitable injection points
        Integration with CI/CD pipeline: Configure Crlfuzz in automated security testing workflow, parse results programmatically for continuous vulnerability assessment
        WAF detection avoidance: Implement custom User-Agent strings, rotate IPs, and adjust request timing to bypass security controls
        Parameter fuzzing combination: Combine CRLF scanning with parameter fuzzing by using multiple payload types and encoding methods
        Response chain analysis: Follow HTTP redirect chains using '--follow-redirects', analyze full response paths for injection points
        Custom regex matching: Apply '-mr' flag with specific regex patterns to identify unique vulnerability signatures
        Domain scope control: Use '-d' flag to limit scanning to specific domains/subdomains while spidering target application
        TLS configuration testing: Test CRLF injection points with various SSL/TLS configurations using '--insecure' flag
        Authentication handling: Configure tool with session tokens/cookies to test authenticated endpoints for CRLF vulnerabilities
        Output format manipulation: Generate structured output (JSON/CSV) for integration with other security tools and reporting systems
        Batch job scheduling: Set up automated periodic scans with different configurations for continuous monitoring
        API endpoint discovery: Combine with API discovery tools, scan discovered endpoints for CRLF vulnerabilities in REST/GraphQL APIs
        Cloud service testing: Configure scanning for cloud-hosted applications with specific considerations for AWS, Azure, GCP environments
        Mobile API testing: Test backend APIs of mobile applications by intercepting and reproducing API calls with CRLF injection attempts
        Custom encoding chains: Chain multiple encoding methods (URL, Base64, hex) to create complex CRLF payloads bypassing filters
        Response splitting exploitation: Leverage identified CRLF vulnerabilities for HTTP response splitting attacks with custom payloads
        Load balancer testing: Test CRLF injection against different backend servers in load-balanced environments
        Cache poisoning setup: Configure payloads specifically for testing web cache poisoning via CRLF injection
        Error-based detection: Implement custom error patterns to identify successful CRLF injections based on application responses
        Protocol-specific testing: Customize scans for different protocols (HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, WebSocket) handling CRLF differently
        Compliance mode: Configure tool to perform scans within compliance boundaries (PCI-DSS, HIPAA) with appropriate documentation
