      Radare2 - Open source reverse engineering framework
        Binary Analysis Procedure: Use r2 CLI to perform static analysis of target binaries, identify functions/strings/imports, generate disassembly, and create visual graphs for control flow analysis
        Debugging and Dynamic Analysis: Attach to running processes using r2's debugger, set breakpoints, analyze memory, and monitor program execution in real-time
        Patch Binary Files: Modify binary code using r2's write mode to alter program behavior, bypass security checks, or inject malicious code
        Extract Embedded Resources: Use r2 to identify and extract embedded files, strings, or cryptographic keys from target binaries
        Analyze Malware Samples: Reverse engineer malware samples to understand functionality, identify C2 servers, and extract configuration data
        Identify Security Vulnerabilities: Analyze binary for common vulnerabilities like buffer overflows, format string bugs, and poor encryption implementations
        Defeat Anti-debugging: Use r2's features to bypass anti-debugging techniques and analyze protected binaries
        Script Development: Create custom r2pipe scripts for automated analysis and exploitation of identified vulnerabilities
        Memory Forensics: Analyze process memory dumps to extract sensitive information or recover encryption keys
        Firmware Analysis: Extract and analyze embedded device firmware to identify vulnerabilities or modification points
        Cross-Platform Binary Analysis: Use r2 to analyze binaries from different architectures (ARM, MIPS, x86) and operating systems for vulnerabilities and exploitation
        Shared Library Analysis: Reverse engineer shared libraries (.dll, .so) to understand API calls, function exports, and identify hook points
        Kernel Module Analysis: Analyze kernel modules and drivers to identify privileged operations and potential exploit vectors
        Graph-based Navigation: Use visual mode to analyze complex control flows, identify loops, and understand program logic through graph visualization
        Cryptographic Implementation Analysis: Identify and analyze cryptographic functions, extract keys, and assess implementation weaknesses
        Code Emulation: Use ESIL (Evaluable Strings Intermediate Language) to emulate code execution and analyze behavior without running the binary
        Binary Diffing: Compare different versions of binaries to identify patches, changes, and potential vulnerability fixes
        Shellcode Analysis: Extract, analyze, and modify shellcode using r2's shellcode specific features
        Format Parsing: Analyze various file formats (PE, ELF, Mach-O) to understand structure and identify manipulation points
        Symbolic Execution: Use r2ghidra decompiler integration for advanced static analysis and symbolic execution
        WebUI Analysis: Use r2web interface for remote analysis and collaboration on binary analysis projects
        Hardware Bridge Integration: Use r2 with hardware debugging tools for analyzing embedded systems and IoT devices
        Deobfuscation: Apply r2's analysis capabilities to handle obfuscated code, decode strings, and unpack protected binaries
        Type Analysis: Reconstruct complex data structures and class hierarchies in compiled code using r2's type system
        API Hooking: Implement runtime API hooks for monitoring and intercepting function calls during dynamic analysis
        Android APK Analysis: Analyze Android applications, extract DEX files, and reverse engineer mobile malware
        Python Integration: Develop custom analysis tools using r2pipe Python bindings for automated vulnerability scanning
        Time-travel Debugging: Use reverse debugging capabilities to track program state changes and analyze complex bugs
        Code Coverage Analysis: Track execution paths during dynamic analysis to identify unexplored code regions
        Zero-day Research: Systematically analyze binaries for previously unknown vulnerabilities using r2's analysis features
