      Flawfinder - C/C++ source code scanner
        Basic source code scan: Install Flawfinder, navigate to target directory, run 'flawfinder .' for recursive scanning of all C/C++ files
        Generate HTML report: Execute scan with '--html --context' options to create detailed vulnerability report with source code context
        Custom ruleset deployment: Modify ~/.flawfinderrc configuration file to add custom security rules and vulnerability patterns
        Focused security scan: Use '--minlevel=4' to identify only high-risk vulnerabilities related to buffer overflows, format strings, and race conditions
        CI/CD integration: Implement Flawfinder in automated build pipeline with '--quiet --csv' for machine-readable output and fail builds on critical findings
        Differential analysis: Use '--patch' option to analyze changes between code versions for new security vulnerabilities
        False positive filtering: Configure using '--falsepositive' and customize rules to reduce noise in scan results
        Targeted function analysis: Use '--functions' option to specifically examine security-critical functions and their implementations
        Data flow analysis: Enable '--dataonly' option to track potentially dangerous data handling in the codebase
        Compliance scanning: Configure scan parameters to check for specific security standard violations (CERT, CWE) using predefined rulesets
        Incremental scanning: Set up cron jobs to periodically scan only modified files using '--followdotdir' and timestamp tracking
        Multi-threading scan: Utilize '--processes' option with specified number of CPU cores for parallel processing of large codebases
        Severity-based triage: Implement custom scoring system using '--singleline' and grep to sort vulnerabilities by risk level
        Cross-reference analysis: Combine Flawfinder output with other tools (like cppcheck) using '--loadhash' for comprehensive vulnerability assessment
        Whitelist management: Create and maintain exception lists using '--allowlist' for known safe patterns and functions
        Historical trending: Set up database storage of scan results to track security metrics over time using '--savehash'
        Library validation: Focus scanning on third-party library integration points using '--inputs' option
        Custom pattern matching: Define regex patterns in configuration to identify organization-specific security anti-patterns
        Immediate fix validation: Use '--diff' option in combination with real-time editing to verify vulnerability remediation
        Export for bug tracking: Format output using '--template' for direct integration with issue tracking systems
        Embedded system analysis: Configure scanning parameters for resource-constrained and bare-metal code using '--neverignore' option
        Binary correlation: Combine source scanning with binary analysis by mapping Flawfinder outputs to compiled code segments
        Custom vulnerability database: Build and maintain local database of vulnerability patterns specific to target architecture
        Sandbox deployment: Set up isolated environment for scanning potentially malicious code with restricted permissions
        Remote scanning: Configure secure remote execution of Flawfinder through SSH with distributed scanning architecture
        Emergency audit: Perform rapid triage scanning with '--immediate' for incident response scenarios
        Legacy code analysis: Adapt scanning parameters for older C/C++ codebases with deprecated functions
        Dependency chain scanning: Create recursive scanning workflow for entire dependency trees with custom depth controls
        Targeted exploit development: Focus scanning on specific vulnerability classes to identify exploitable conditions
        Compliance reporting: Generate customized reports matching specific security certification requirements (ISO, SOC2, etc.)
