      IDA Pro - Advanced multi-processor disassembler and debugger
        Analyze malware samples by disassembling executables, identifying key functions, creating function graphs, and extracting strings and cryptographic constants to understand malware behavior and develop countermeasures
        Reverse engineer software security mechanisms by analyzing control flow, identifying authentication routines, and locating encryption implementations to bypass protection features
        Create IDA Python scripts to automate binary analysis, decode obfuscated strings, identify API calls, and extract specific code patterns across multiple samples
        Use debugging capabilities to analyze runtime behavior, set breakpoints at critical functions, and monitor memory/register states to understand program execution flow
        Generate and modify function signatures using FLIRT to identify library functions in stripped binaries and speed up analysis of similar samples
        Analyze firmware and embedded system binaries by loading appropriate processor modules, defining memory segments, and reconstructing data structures
        Patch binary files by modifying assembly instructions, updating jump conditions, and adjusting data values to alter program behavior or bypass security checks
        Perform cross-references analysis to track data flow, identify function calls, and understand program logic for vulnerability discovery
        Export analysis results including annotations, comments, and renamed functions to share findings or import into other analysis tools
        Use decompiler plugins to convert assembly code to pseudo-C code for easier understanding of program logic and vulnerability identification
        Create custom IDA plugins using SDK to extend functionality, add new analysis capabilities, or integrate with other reverse engineering tools
        Use remote debugging features to analyze malware in isolated environments, attach to running processes, and debug network-enabled malware
        Analyze kernel drivers and rootkits by loading appropriate symbols, identifying system calls, and mapping driver interactions with the OS
        Perform differential binary analysis by comparing multiple versions of software to identify patches, security fixes, and potential vulnerabilities
        Reverse engineer proprietary protocols and file formats by analyzing parsing routines, data structures, and communication patterns
        Use type libraries and RTTI information to reconstruct complex C++ class hierarchies and understand object-oriented program structures
        Analyze anti-debugging and anti-analysis techniques by identifying code obfuscation, VM detection, and debugger detection routines
