    Container-Specific Malware: Malware targeting container environments and orchestration systems
      Siloscape: First known malware targeting Windows containers and Kubernetes clusters for cryptomining and backdoor deployment
        Exploit container escape vulnerabilities in Windows containers using known CVEs to break out into the host system
        Compromise Kubernetes clusters by targeting exposed Kubelets and API servers through misconfigured endpoints
        Create malicious containers with Siloscape payload and deploy them through compromised Kubernetes credentials
        Establish persistence by creating privileged containers that survive cluster restarts
        Deploy cryptomining software across compromised container nodes using Kubernetes DaemonSets
        Create backdoor accounts and modify Kubernetes RBAC policies for maintaining access
        Use Windows container escape techniques to access the underlying host's credentials and secrets
        Leverage compromised nodes to spread laterally through the Kubernetes cluster
        Modify container runtime configurations to maintain stealth and evade detection
        Exfiltrate sensitive data from containers and Kubernetes secrets through C2 channels
        Modify container networking configurations to establish covert communication channels between compromised nodes
        Deploy rootkits within containers to hide malicious processes and network connections
        Manipulate container image registries to distribute infected container images across the cluster
        Exploit container runtime (containerd, CRI-O) vulnerabilities for privilege escalation
        Create backdoored container images that appear legitimate but contain hidden malicious code
        Modify Kubernetes admission controllers to bypass security policies
        Leverage container orchestration tools (e.g., Helm) to deploy malicious workloads
        Exploit container storage interfaces to gain access to persistent volumes and sensitive data
        Manipulate node labels and taints to control workload placement for optimal cryptomining
        Install kernel-level rootkits on host systems through escaped container contexts
        Exploit Kubernetes scheduler to ensure malicious pods are distributed across specific nodes
        Manipulate container logging systems to hide malicious activity and inject false logs
        Deploy sidecar containers to intercept and modify inter-service communication
        Compromise container service mesh (e.g., Istio) for traffic manipulation and data interception
        Exploit container runtime sandboxes to bypass security controls
        Modify container resource quotas and limits to optimize cryptomining performance
        Inject malicious code into container initialization processes and startup scripts
        Exploit container auto-scaling mechanisms to spread infection to new nodes
        Compromise container network policies to bypass segmentation controls
        Manipulate container health checks to maintain persistence and avoid detection
      Graboid: Self-propagating cryptomining worm targeting Docker containers
        Initial infection through exposed Docker APIs:
        - Scan for exposed Docker daemon ports (2375/2376)
        - Connect to unprotected Docker API endpoints
        - Deploy malicious container images
        Self-propagation mechanism:
        - Download malicious scripts from C2 server
        - Search for other vulnerable Docker hosts
        - Establish new infections on discovered targets
        Cryptomining payload deployment:
        - Pull cryptocurrency mining software
        - Configure mining pool settings
        - Execute mining operations with stolen resources
        Container manipulation:
        - Remove existing containers
        - Disable security features
        - Create privileged containers for persistence
        Resource consumption and impact:
        - Consume CPU resources for mining
        - Degrade host performance
        - Impact container availability
        Evasion techniques:
        - Delete infection traces
        - Modify container logs
        - Use legitimate-looking image names
        - Implement sleep intervals between activities
        Network communication patterns:
        - Establish connections to mining pools
        - Contact C2 servers for updates
        - Use DNS queries for command retrieval
        - Implement fallback communication channels
        Persistence mechanisms:
        - Create scheduled tasks in containers
        - Modify container startup scripts
        - Implement watchdog processes
        - Auto-restart compromised containers
        Data collection and exfiltration:
        - Gather container environment information
        - Collect Docker configuration details
        - Extract sensitive data from mounted volumes
        - Transfer collected data to C2 servers
        Recovery prevention:
        - Modify container configurations
        - Corrupt Docker daemon settings
        - Implement anti-analysis techniques
        - Block security tool installations
      TeamTNT: Malware toolkit targeting exposed Docker and Kubernetes environments for cryptomining
        Scan for exposed Docker APIs and Kubernetes clusters using masscan/zgrab, then exploit unauthenticated endpoints to deploy malicious containers
        Deploy credential stealing scripts targeting AWS credentials, Docker credentials, and SSH keys in container environments
        Use XMRig miner deployment scripts to establish cryptomining operations across compromised container infrastructure
        Establish persistence through cron jobs and systemd services while disabling competing cryptominers
        Deploy network worm capabilities to spread across container networks using exposed APIs and weak credentials
        Install backdoors and rootkits in container images while maintaining stealth through process hiding
