      Kinsing: Container malware focusing on cryptomining and lateral movement in Kubernetes clusters
        Initial compromise through exposed Docker APIs:
        - Scan for exposed Docker daemon ports (2375/2376)
        - Execute malicious container using "docker run" commands
        - Deploy initial Kinsing payload through vulnerable container services
        Credential harvesting and lateral movement:
        - Extract Kubernetes service account tokens from compromised pods
        - Scan for additional exposed services within the cluster
        - Use stolen credentials to access other nodes/namespaces
        Cryptomining deployment procedure:
        - Download mining configuration from C2 server
        - Set up XMRig miner with predefined pool settings
        - Configure resource limits to avoid detection
        - Establish persistence through cronjobs
        Defense evasion techniques:
        - Remove competing malware and cryptominers
        - Disable cloud provider security agents
        - Clear log files and shell history
        - Modify system timestamps
        Kubernetes cluster exploitation:
        - Exploit misconfigured RBAC permissions
        - Create privileged pods for broader access
        - Mount host filesystem for persistence
        - Deploy DaemonSets for cluster-wide infection
        Network communication setup:
        - Establish connections to known C2 domains
        - Configure proxy settings to bypass network restrictions
